WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.313
Why a digest, and why evidence?

2
00:00:02.313 --> 00:00:05.624
Not every notice is a task,
and most shouldn't be.

3
00:00:05.624 --> 00:00:08.275
The digest is how the owners see the rest.

4
00:00:08.275 --> 00:00:11.710
Once a week it lists, for each
provider, the open notices;

5
00:00:11.710 --> 00:00:15.374
every notice that needs action,
with its due date and owner;

6
00:00:15.374 --> 00:00:19.266
the maintenance and changes
starting in the next thirty days;

7
00:00:19.266 --> 00:00:23.388
what's new in the last week; and
what closed or stopped being listed.

8
00:00:23.388 --> 00:00:26.599
It goes to the owners,
not to a shared mailbox.

9
00:00:26.599 --> 00:00:31.393
The Compliance Automation and Day-Two
Operations book gives it a natural home in

10
00:00:31.393 --> 00:00:33.038
reviews that already exist:

11
00:00:33.038 --> 00:00:37.831
the service desk supervisor's weekly review
of the queue, and the change advisory

12
00:00:37.831 --> 00:00:42.338
board's agenda, where the next thirty
days of maintenance and changes belong.

13
00:00:42.338 --> 00:00:44.992
Two numbers from it are
worth watching over time:

14
00:00:44.992 --> 00:00:49.307
notices that needed action and reached
their due date with the task still open,

15
00:00:49.307 --> 00:00:52.825
and notices that were never routed
because no rule matched them.

16
00:00:52.825 --> 00:00:54.363
That's design guidance.

17
00:00:54.363 --> 00:00:58.291
The evidence is the other
half: what the ISSO keeps,

18
00:00:58.291 --> 00:01:01.030
recorded so a change or a gap shows.
